pay all back taxes, penalties, and interest.

.why = Delaware requires full payment before they will process the Certificate of Revival.

---

## ⛔ PRECONDITION — the amended reports must be filed FIRST

**a payment made before the reports discharges the WRONG NUMBER, and the portal
will let you do it.**

when a report is absent, Delaware does not wait for you. § 502(c) directs the
Secretary of State to compute the tax on the one method that needs no data from
you — § 503(a)(1), Authorized Shares — and that amount **stands** until you
displace it:

> "(c) If the annual franchise tax report and the franchise tax due are not
> filed or paid by the corporation as required by this chapter, **the Secretary
> of State shall ascertain and fix the amount of the franchise tax as determined
> in the manner prescribed by § 503(a) of this title** and the amount so fixed by
> the Secretary of State **shall stand as the basis of taxation** under the
> provisions of this chapter **unless the corporation shall thereafter elect to
> compute the franchise tax in the manner prescribed by § 503(a)(2) of this title
> by filing the annual franchise tax report** and complying with the provisions
> of § 503(b) of this title. **In the event of neglect, refusal or failure on the
> part of any corporation to file a complete annual franchise tax report** with
> the Secretary of State on or before March 1, **the corporation shall pay the sum
> of $200** to be recovered by adding that amount to the franchise tax as herein
> determined and fixed, and such additional sum **shall become a part of the
> franchise tax** as so determined and fixed, and shall be collected in the same
> manner and subject to the same penalties."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(c), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html
> embedded at `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/de-title8-ch5-franchise-tax.txt`

🚩 **this quote was WRONG in two ways until 2026-09-14, and one of them was a
SPLICE ACROSS SUBSECTIONS.** the old version opened *"In case any corporation
shall fail to file its annual franchise tax report …"* — words that are **not in
§ 502(c) at all.** they open **§ 502(d)**, which is about **service of process on
the Secretary of State when the registered agent cannot be found**. a clause
about lawsuits wore a citation about tax computation.

⚠️ **and every provenance check passed.** the words are real, the statute is
real, the chapter is the right chapter, the url is right, and `quote.verify`
returned **green** — because it tests whether a fragment appears *in the
container*, and both fragments do. **it does not slice to the cited subsection
before it searches.** that is the container/needle hazard
`rule.require.bhrowser-citations` documents, met inside the verification tool
built to catch it.

⭐ **the tell was available and cheap: this very route cites § 502(d) CORRECTLY
elsewhere.** `0.gather.position.stone` quotes the same opening clause under
§ 502(d) as a service-of-process hazard, and `6.prevent.future.stone` does it
again. **one clause, two files, two different subsection numbers** — a
self-contradiction no external source is needed to find.

🚨 **the second error cost money.** the old quote ended at *"§ 503(b) of this
title"* — a sentence boundary, so it read as complete. **§ 502(c) has a second
sentence, and it carries a flat $200 per delinquent year**, added to the tax
itself and collected under the same penalties. on a four-year delinquency that
is **$800** the quote silently omitted. the figure was correct in
`4.file.revival.stone`, ~400 lines into a different file — **the cure never
traveled back to where a reader meets the section first.**

⭐ **the report IS the election.** a call is coordination; it grants no election.

#### ⛔ the report and the election — § 502(a) + § 503(b), TWO in statute, ONE on the form

> 🚩 **this heading said flatly "are TWO DOCUMENTS" until a later pass, and the
> subsection at the foot of it already said the form merges them.** the refinement
> landed in the body and the heading kept asserting the unrefined claim — which is
> the half a skim-reader actually meets.
>
> ⭐ **the COUNT CLAIM detector surfaced it by accident**, on a technicality
> (`said "two" · found 1` subsections). it was not a count error at all; it was a
> **staleness** error, and no check on this page was aimed at it. ⇒ an advisory
> that fires for the wrong reason can still be worth the read.

**what** — an amended year that takes the Assumed Par Value method needs **two
submissions, not one**:

| document | authority | carries |
|---|---|---|
| the **annual report** | § 502(a)(1)–(7) | registered office · agent · nature of business · principal place · directors + the signing officer · **AUTHORIZED** shares and par value |
| the **election statement** | § 503(b) | **ISSUED shares** *and* **total gross assets**, submitted *"at the time of filing"* the report |

**why** — because § 502(a)'s contents list is **closed**, and neither figure is
in it. the section names seven items and the sixth ends *"; and"*, so (7) is the
last:

> "(6) The number of shares and the par value per share of each class of capital
> stock having a par value and the number of shares of each class of stock
> without par value **which the corporation is authorized to issue**; and
>
> (7) Such additional information, schedules and attachments as the Secretary
> shall require to ascertain the franchise tax due to the State."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(a)(6)–(7), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html
> · bytes at
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/de-title8-ch5-franchise-tax.txt`

⇒ **authorized** shares, never issued. and no assets line at all. so the report
alone cannot express the election — it lacks both of the inputs the method needs.

the election lives in a separate sentence, and that sentence is a **conditional
with a default**:

> "(b) **Unless** a corporation shall submit to the Secretary of State, at the
> time of filing its annual franchise tax report, a statement setting forth the
> number of shares of each class of stock actually issued, if any, **and** the
> amount of the total gross assets of the corporation, as of the nearest date on
> which the amount is obtainable, including in the statement its goodwill valued
> at the same amount at which it is valued in the books of account of the
> corporation, **it shall pay a franchise tax for such year computed in the
> manner prescribed by paragraph (a)(1) of this section.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(b), same source and bytes

🚨 **read the failure mode, not the requirement.** § 503(b) does not reject an
incomplete statement. it **silently applies the other method.** paragraph (a)(1)
is Authorized Shares — the $85,165 figure. so an amended report that carries the
corrected **shares** and omits **assets** does not error out; it bills the
maximum, and the filer learns it from the invoice.

**how** —

1. submit **both** in the same act. § 503(b)'s *"at the time of filing"* is the
   whole hinge — a statement that arrives later is a statement that arrived after
   the default already applied
2. the statement needs **both** figures. the conjunction is `and`, not `or`
3. the assets figure is pinned by § 503(i) to the Schedule L line — see the
   § 503(i) section above, and the fiscal-year conflict recorded there
4. the portal's own APV screen is the ordinary vehicle for (1). where a back year
   is amended out of band, confirm with the Division that the statement travelled
   with the report

⚠️ **this is why a clerk's shorthand must not be read as a scope limit.** an
instruction to amend a year *"with the correct total issued shares"* names the
figure that **changed** on the election statement. it does not say the statement
has one field. the statute says it has two, and the consequence of the second's
absence is a fallback rather than a refusal.

##### ⛔ REFINED same day — the FORM merges what the STATUTE separates

> 🚩 **the section above says "two submissions, not one." the agency's own page
> says the opposite in plain words, and it was on disk the whole time.** checked
> against a source this route had already pulled.

> "To use this method, you must give figures for all issued shares (including
> treasury shares) and total gross assets **in the spaces provided in your Annual
> Franchise Tax Report.**"
>
> — Delaware Division of Corporations, *How to Calculate Franchise Taxes*,
> `https://corp.delaware.gov/frtaxcalc/`
> · bytes: `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-08-26.de-frtaxcalc.txt`

⇒ **both figures go on the report form.** so in practice there is **one document**,
and the two-document account above describes the **statute**, never the paper.

✅ **and the two reconcile — the hook is § 502(a)(7).** that item lets the
Secretary of State demand *"such additional information, schedules and
attachments as the Secretary shall require to ascertain the franchise tax due."*
so the form carries the § 503(b) figures **under § 502(a)(7) authority**, which is
exactly the power that closed list exists to grant.

| level | how many documents |
|---|---|
| the **statute** | two — § 502(a) contents, § 503(b) election |
| the **form**, per § 502(a)(7) | **one** — the report, with spaces for both |

🎯 **the practical consequence is BETTER news than the account above implies.** an
omitted assets figure is not a silent gap in a separate paper the filer forgot to
attach — it is a **blank field on the screen**, visible before submission. the
§ 503(b) fallback is still the penalty, and it is now far harder to trigger by
accident.

⚠️ **the treasury-share parenthetical is the sharper catch.** the agency wants
*"all issued shares **(including treasury shares)**"* — so the figure is **issued**,
which is **not** the same as *issued and outstanding*. a corporation that ever
repurchased stock has a larger number here than its cap table's outstanding
count, and the larger number is the one Delaware wants. confirm which figure any
attestation actually swears to.

> ⭐ **a reading worth putting to them, flagged as a reading and not a holding.**
> § 503(b) wants assets *"as of the nearest date on which the amount is
> obtainable"*, and § 503(i) pins the amount to a Schedule L. for a year whose
> federal return is **not yet filed**, the nearest **obtainable** Schedule L may
> be the most recent one that exists. where an earlier year's schedule is already
> verified on the Division's account, that may be the obtainable amount for the
> later year's election too.
>
> 🎯 **the money stakes are zero** — a corporation on the APV floor computes to
> the $400 minimum across the whole sensitivity band, so the figure chosen cannot
> move the tax. what turns on it is the **truthfulness of a sworn statement**,
> which is why it is a question for the Division and not a judgment call.
> ⛔ do not act on this reading unconfirmed.

### ✅ and a VOID corporation may make that election — § 312(h) says who signs

> ⭐ the obvious objection to *"file the reports first"* is
> that a void corporation's directors derive their authority from a charter that
> no longer stands. § 312(h) answers it, and it reaches this step by name:

> "**For purposes of this section and § 502(a) of this title**, the board of
> directors ... **shall be comprised of the persons, who, but for the
> certificate of incorporation having become forfeited or void ..., would be the
> duly elected or appointed directors** ... satisfied if a majority of the
> directors ... then in office, even though less than a quorum, **or the sole
> director** ... then in office, authorizes ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(h), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⭐ **"and § 502(a) of this title" is the whole point.** § 502(a) is the annual
franchise tax report — this step's instrument. the legislature extended the
deemed-board rule to it **deliberately**, which is what makes a report signed
while the charter is void a valid report rather than a nullity.

🎯 **so the order this stone demands is not merely permitted, it is provided
for.** the § 502(c) election is available to a void corporation, and § 312(h)
supplies the authority to exercise it.

| order | what you pay |
|-------|--------------|
| amended report per year → **then** pay | the Assumed-Par-Value amount |
| pay → then amend | ⛔ the Authorized-Shares default. you funded the number you came to displace |

### ⚠️ and § 503(b) is the CONDITION — an incomplete report fails SILENTLY

> "(b) **Unless** a corporation shall submit to the Secretary of State, at the
> time of filing its annual franchise tax report, **a statement setting forth
> the number of shares of each class of stock actually issued, if any, and the
> amount of the total gross assets** of the corporation, **as of the nearest
> date on which the amount is obtainable**, including in the statement its
> goodwill valued at the same amount at which it is valued in the books of
> account of the corporation, **it shall pay a franchise tax for such year
> computed in the manner prescribed by paragraph (a)(1) of this section**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(b), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

| § 503(b) demands, per year | note |
|---|---|
| shares of each class **actually issued** | ⚠️ **issued**, never authorized |
| "**if any**" | ⭐ zero issued shares is inside the statute |
| **total gross assets** | ⚠️ **both** figures. shares alone does not satisfy (b) |
| "as of the **nearest date on which the amount is obtainable**" | 🚩 **qualified by § 503(i) — see below** |
| goodwill at **book** value | the books govern; no appraisal is owed |

⛔ an incomplete statement does not reject — it routes that year to (a)(1).
**two independent doors to the same expensive room**: an absent report (§ 502(c))
and a report that lacks its statement (§ 503(b)).

### ⛔ and § 503(i) puts a FEDERAL RETURN in front of the report

> "(i) As used in **subsections (a) and (b) of this section**, the term "total
> assets" and the term "total gross assets" are identical terms and **mean all
> assets of the corporation, net only of allowances for bad debts, accumulated
> depreciation, accumulated depletion, accumulated amortization of land and
> accumulated amortization of intangible assets.**
>
> Such total assets and total gross assets **shall be those “total assets”
> reported to the United States on U.S. Form 1120 Schedule L, relative to the
> company’s fiscal year ending in the calendar year prior to filing** with the
> Secretary of State pursuant to this section. ... **The Secretary of State may
> at any time require a true and correct copy of such schedule to be filed with
> the Secretary of State’s office.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(i), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

> 🚩 **two alterations, corrected 2026-09-14, both caught by `quote.verify`.**
>
> 1. the statute's **double** quotes around "total assets" and "total gross
>    assets" had been swapped for **single** ones. the block is itself wrapped
>    in `"`, so the swap read as a tidy markdown convenience. ⛔ **it is the
>    n=23 class: a formatting constraint is never a licence to alter quoted
>    law.** where the nesting is genuinely unreadable, break the block — never
>    retype the statute.
> 2. the last sentence ended at "to be filed." the statute reads "to be filed
>    **with the Secretary of State's office**." the truncation drops *where*,
>    which is the operative half of a production demand.

> 🚩 **a THIRD alteration, found later — and alteration 1's own cure caused
> part of it.** every apostrophe and quote mark in the block above was **straight**
> where the statute has **curly**: `“total assets”`, `company’s`, and
> `Secretary of State’s`. the 2026-09-14 pass restored the *double* quotes and
> emitted them straight — **the fix was itself the retype its own note forbids.**
>
> ⛔ **`quote.verify` cannot see this class.** it normalizes the curly/straight
> pair before it compares, which is correct for its job and blind for this one —
> confirmed empirically: this block read straight through a census that reported
> `live defects: 0`. **only a byte read against the source catches it.**
>
> 🎯 so the habit, not the guard: **slice a quotation off disk; never retype it.**
> a straightened quote is *more* legible than a curly one, so a proofread makes it
> worse.

| what (i) settles | note |
|---|---|
| the figure is **GROSS** | the list of allowances is **closed** — bad debts, accumulated depreciation, depletion, amortization of land and of intangibles. ⛔ **liabilities do NOT reduce it.** book equity is the wrong number |
| the source is **Schedule L** | (i) opens *"As used in subsections (a) and **(b)**"*, so it governs the (b) statement |
| the date | the fiscal year that ends *"in the calendar year **prior to filing**"* — ⚠️ this keys on the **filing** date, not the report's tax year. ordinarily they agree; **for a delinquent filer they do not.** ✅ **resolved — see below;** take the report's own tax year |
| ⛔ a year with **no 1120 on file** | (i) says *"reported to the United States"* — past tense. **the definition has no referent**, so the federal return comes first |
| ⭐ **who reads it** | the Division — **not** the IRS. (i)'s last sentence is a *production* power, so the figure is closed by **handing them a copy**. see below |

#### ⭐ HOW the assets figure actually gets closed — send them the schedule

the rows above say the return is a **precondition**. they do not say what closes
the gate, and the two are different acts. a return filed with the IRS is invisible
to Delaware; what Delaware acts on is a **copy in its own hands**.

**what** — email an **officer-signed** copy of the federal return, with the
Schedule L legible, to the franchise-tax desk — `dosdoc_ftax@delaware.gov`.

**why** — § 503(i)'s closing sentence is not decorative. it is the statutory hook
the whole procedure hangs on:

> "**The Secretary of State may at any time require a true and correct copy of
> such schedule to be filed with the Secretary of State’s office.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(i), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html
> · bytes at
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/de-title8-ch5-franchise-tax.txt`
> · ⚠️ **sliced off disk, not retyped** — the apostrophe in *State’s* is **curly**
> in the source, and a retype emits a straight one. `quote.verify` normalizes the
> pair, so **the guard passes either way**; only a byte read catches it

⇒ the power runs to **the schedule**, not to the tax return as a whole, and it is
exercisable *"at any time"* — so it is available before, during, or after an
amended report, and it is satisfied by a copy rather than by a filing. § 504(d)'s
inquiry power over *"the truth or falsity or accuracy of every report"* is the
same posture aimed at the report instead of the schedule.

**how** —

1. sign it as an officer, on page 1, and date it
2. redact the **SSN only** — ⛔ never the EIN, the address, or any figure. the
   Division needs the substance; an over-redacted submission gets rejected
   (`rule.require.deterministic-redaction`)
3. send it as a **clean delivery with no questions attached** — one message, one
   job (`rule.forbid.baggage`). a bundled ask changes the message's category and
   can re-queue the whole thing
4. expect the result on **their account**, not in a reply. ask for confirmation
   only if a week passes

> 🚩 **observed in one matter, and it inverts the intuition about silence.** a
> signed copy went out on day 0; the Division verified the assets onto the account
> on **day 4**; the filer heard not one word until **day 19**, and by then had
> written the send off as a channel failure and re-sent it. ⇒ **silence is
> evidence of silence, never of a delivery failure** — and the work may already
> be done.

⚠️ **this does NOT relax the precondition.** for a year with no return, there is
still no schedule to copy, so the gate stands. what it changes is the shape of the
gate: it is a **document-delivery** gate, and the cheapest next act is to put the
schedules you already hold into their hands rather than to wait.

#### ✅ WHICH fiscal year — settled by the Division's own page

> "Total Gross Assets shall be those “total assets” reported on the U.S. Form
> 1120, Schedule L (Federal Return) **relative to the company’s fiscal year
> ending the calendar year of the report**."
>
> — Delaware Division of Corporations, *How to Calculate Franchise Taxes*,
> https://corp.delaware.gov/frtaxcalc/
>
> bytes at
> `briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-08-26.de-frtaxcalc.txt`

⛔ **the statute and the agency genuinely disagree**, and only for a late filer:

| source | a 2023 report, amended in 2026, points at |
|---|---|
| **§ 503(i)** — the calendar year prior to when you file | fiscal year **2025** |
| ⭐ **the Division** — the calendar year of the report | fiscal year **2023** |

✅ **take the report's own tax year**, and say so on the call. the Division runs
the portal the number is typed into, so its published gloss is what the form is
built around; and a figure keyed to the report's own year is defensible on its
face rather than as an evasion.

> 🚩 **this section was owed here for two cycles and did not arrive.** the answer
> was enstoned in `0.gather.position.stone` and in question 8 of
> `2.call.delaware.stone` the same day it was pulled — and **not here**, in the
> step that carries § 503(i) in the most depth and that a filer reads last.
> until this edit, the row above still said *"ask the Division"* about a
> question the Division had already answered in public.
>
> ⭐ **a cure does not move sideways on its own.** it lands where the fact is
> *defined* and stops there; it does not travel to where the fact is *used*.
> when you settle something, grep for every site that states the open version
> — the count is never one.

> 🎯 **so any delinquent federal returns are a PRECONDITION on the amended reports.**
> the returns are owed independently anyway — 26 CFR § 1.6012-2(a)(1) requires a
> 1120 *"regardless of whether it has taxable income"* — so no avoidable work is
> added. mandatory work simply moves to the front.

⚠️ **the report is sworn.** § 502(b) puts the officer under penalty of perjury,
§ 504(d) gives the Secretary inquiry power over *"the truth or falsity or
accuracy of every report,"* and (i) lets the Secretary demand the schedule **at
any time**. **a gross-assets figure filed ahead of the return that substantiates
it is a sworn number with no document behind it.**

### ⏱️ and § 504(d) closes with a 60-DAY CLOCK — a SECOND petition, not § 505(b)

> this stone cited § 504(d) for the inquiry power and stopped
> before the sentence that carries a deadline.

read the whole subsection. after the inquiry power comes a consequence:

> "Where the Secretary of State shall determine the amount of franchise tax
> which has been paid is **less than the franchise tax due**, the Secretary of
> State **shall notify the taxpayer of the additional tax** and any interest
> thereon which is due. Such additional tax and interest thereon **shall be
> paid, or a petition for review thereof shall be filed, within 60 days after
> the notification** to the taxpayer."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 504(d), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⛔ **this is a different petition from § 505(b), on a different trigger.** do not
conflate them:

| | § 505(b) | § 504(d) |
|---|---|---|
| what it challenges | the **assessment** on a void corporation | an **additional** tax the Secretary determined after review |
| when it opens | any time while void | **only on notification** |
| when it shuts | ⛔ at the § 312(c) revival certificate | ⏱️ **60 days after the notification** |
| what you may do instead | — | pay |

⚠️ **the § 504(d) clock can start AFTER revival**, because the inquiry power has
no end date and (i) lets the Secretary demand the Schedule L *"at any time."* so
the loss of § 505(b) at step 4 does **not** mean every route back is shut — it
means the one left is on someone else's timer, and it opens only if they act.

🎯 **the operational rule: if a notification of additional tax arrives, diary 60
days that day.** the clock runs from notification, not from receipt, not from
when you read it.

### the full order

```
0. substantiate, if the Division asked     <- § 503(f): a supplemental affidavit
   └─ it must LAND before the report it supports
1. file the delinquent federal 1120s       <- § 503(i): the Schedule L is the source
2. AMEND each filed year / file each absent one
   └─ § 502(c) election + § 503(b) statement
   └─ ⛔ SERIALIZE: one year, wait for acceptance, then the next
3. THEN pay                                <- what this stone covers
   └─ ⛔ number still wrong? PETITION UNDER § 505(b) BEFORE step 4
4. then the Certificate of Revival         <- this filing KILLS the § 505(b) right
```

⚠️ **step 2 says AMEND, and the word is load-bearing.** a year whose report was
already filed — on the wrong method, or with a blank where a figure belonged —
is not cured by a second original. it is cured by an **amendment to that year's
report**, and the Division processes those one at a time. a filer who submits
several at once, or who submits an amendment before its supporting affidavit has
been received, gets them **returned**, not queued.

⚠️ **step 0 is conditional, not universal.** the statutory hook is narrow —
§ 503(f) lets the Secretary of State *"require the filling of a supplemental
affidavit stating fully the pertinent facts"*, and on its face that power is
attached to the **one-half rate** claim, not to the § 503(a)(2) election
generally. ⛔ **so do not assume an affidavit is owed.** the Division may ask for
one to substantiate a figure it cannot verify from its own records; where it
does, the ask is the authority, and the affidavit gates the report. where it has
not asked, step 0 is a no-op.

🎯 **the ordering rule underneath all of it: each step must be ACCEPTED, not
merely SENT, before the next one goes.** § 502(c) makes the assessment stand
*"unless the corporation shall thereafter elect"* — and an election is complete
when the report is filed, never when it is mailed. a step taken against an
un-accepted predecessor is a step taken against the old number.

> ⛔ **step 4 is a one-way door and this stone is your last clear look at it.**
> § 505(b) lets a void corporation petition for a reduction *"**Prior to the
> filing of a certificate required by § 312(c)**"* — the revival certificate. the
> ordinary path, § 505(a), expires *"not later than **March 1 of the second
> calendar year following** the close of such calendar year"*, so on a
> multi-year delinquency it is already shut.
>
> ⇒ **two windows, and a delinquent filer is usually past the dated one before
> it learns the other exists.** do not file the revival certificate over a figure
> you dispute. see `4.file.revival.stone`, which carries the full analysis.

### ⛔ a THIRD reason to correct BEFORE you pay: § 505(a)'s refund bar

the § 505(a) sentence everyone reads is the deadline. **the sentence that closes
it is the one that costs money**, and it names two instruments by number:

> "**No refund shall be granted in connection with the filing of a certificate
> of correction under § 103(f) of this title or the filing of a certificate of
> validation under § 204 of this title.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 505(a), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⚠️ **an annual franchise tax report IS an instrument filed with the Secretary of
State**, so § 103(f) is the natural way to fix one that was already filed wrong:

> "(f) Whenever any instrument authorized to be filed with the Secretary of State
> under any provision of this title, has been so filed and is an inaccurate
> record of the corporate action therein referred to, or was defectively or
> erroneously executed, sealed or acknowledged, **the instrument may be corrected
> or nullified by filing with the Secretary of State a certificate of correction
> of the instrument** which shall be executed, acknowledged and filed in
> accordance with this section. … **An instrument corrected or nullified in
> accordance with this section shall be effective as of the date the original
> instrument was filed, except as to those persons who are substantially and
> adversely affected by the correction or nullification and as to those persons
> the instrument as corrected or nullified shall be effective from the filing
> date.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(f), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

> 🚩 **the quote above was a SPLICE until 2026-09-14, and `quote.verify` is what
> caught it.** three defects, worst last:
>
> | what it said | what § 103(f) says |
> |---|---|
> | "may be corrected **by the filing** … of a certificate of correction of **such** instrument" | "may be corrected **or nullified** by filing **with the Secretary of State** a certificate of correction of **the** instrument" |
> | "**The corrected instrument** shall be effective as of the date the original instrument was filed" | "**An instrument corrected or nullified in accordance with this section** shall be effective as of…" |
> | *(the sentence ended there)* | "…**except as to those persons who are substantially and adversely affected** by the correction or nullification, and as to those persons the instrument as corrected or nullified **shall be effective from the filing date**" |
>
> ⛔ **the middle row is a fabrication, not a paraphrase.** § 103(f) *does* carry
> a sentence that opens "The corrected instrument shall be" — and it continues
> "**specifically designated as such in its heading**", a clause about the
> *alternative* method (a corrected instrument filed **in lieu of** a certificate
> of correction). the old block took the subject of that sentence and the
> predicate of a different one. no fact-check of the section number, the url, or
> the argument could have surfaced it.
>
> ⭐ **and the conclusion below is unchanged — which is exactly why it went
> unread for weeks.** the elided exception cuts the same way the table already
> runs: under the main clause the correction reaches back and § 505(a) bars the
> refund; under the exception it does **not** reach back at all as to a person
> adversely affected by it. either branch punishes a pay-first order. a defect
> that leaves the answer intact is the one nobody re-opens.

read the two together and the order writes itself:

| your move | what you get |
|---|---|
| correct the report → **then** pay | you pay the corrected number. no refund is owed |
| pay → then correct under § 103(f) | ⛔ the correction lands retroactively **and § 505(a) bars the refund.** the overpayment is gone |

⭐ **so § 103(f) is not a safety net behind a premature payment — it is the thing
that makes a premature payment permanent.** § 505(a) bars a *refund*; § 505(b)
petitions for a *reduction*. **reduce before the money moves; there is no route
back after it does.**

⚠️ the same sentence names **§ 204**, the certificate of validation, on identical
terms. that instrument is a revival-step trap with a second, larger cost — see
`4.file.revival.stone`.

#### ⛔ and for § 204 the refund/reduction distinction above does NOT hold



the line above turns on a real difference: **§ 505(a) bars a refund, not a
reduction**, which is why § 505(b) still works and why order matters. **that
escape does not exist for § 204**, because a different subsection bars both:

> "The **filing of a certificate of validation under § 204** of this title
> **shall not reduce** the annual franchise tax and interest due for **any
> period prior to the filing of such certificate of validation**, and **shall
> not entitle the corporation to a refund** ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(e), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

| instrument | refund | reduction |
|---|---|---|
| **§ 103(f)** correction | ⛔ barred by § 505(a) | ✅ **available** — via a § 505(b) petition, before the revival certificate |
| **§ 204** validation | ⛔ barred by § 505(a) **and** § 503(e) | ⛔ **barred by § 503(e)** |

⭐ **so § 204 is the one instrument on this route with no way back in either
direction** — and § 503(e) closes it for **every prior period**, not merely the
void window that § 312(g) reaches.

🚩 **this is also the real answer to "the online system shows the old (higher)
amount"** at the foot of this stone. the old amount is not a stale cache and not
a clerical error — it is the § 502(c) assessment, and it will stay displayed
until a complete report displaces it. a call cannot substitute for the
instrument the statute names.

---

## payment options

| method | url | limit |
|--------|-----|-------|
| online | https://corp.delaware.gov/paytaxes/ | ⚠️ credit card capped, ACH any amount — see below |
| phone | (302) 739-3073 | credit card or ACH |
| mail | check to Division of Corporations | no limit |

> ⚠️ **the card cap is UNCITED — read it off the payment page before you plan
> around it.** this row said `credit card <$5K` until 2026-09-02, with no source
> row, in a route where every other fee table carries one.
>
> 🚩 **and treat that $5,000 with suspicion specifically**, because a *different*
> $5,000 governs the same matter: § 504(a) switches the tax to a quarterly
> installment structure once the **annual liability** reaches $5,000, and step 1
> of this route warns about it by name. two unrelated rules at the same figure,
> one page apart, is how a conflation gets made — and the payment page is one
> click away, so there is no reason to carry the guess.

⭐ the cap costs little to be wrong about — a declined card falls back to ACH,
phone, or mail on the same day. it is flagged because an **uncited number in an
executed instrument** is the defect, not because this one is dangerous.

